INSERT INTO _s478 VALUES
(4448,'T','4-maccabees',10,1,'4-maccabees',10,1,$n$*And fear not them which kill the body, but are not able to kill the soul: but rather fear him which is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.* (Matthew 10:28) *And when he had bravely met his glorious death, the third son was brought forward and was earnestly entreated by many to taste and so to save himself. But he answered in a loud voice, ‘Are ye ignorant that the same father begat me and my brothers that are dead, and the same mother gave us birth, and in the same doctrines was I brought up? I do not forswear the noble bond of brotherhood. Therefore if ye have any engine of torment, apply it to this body of mine; for my soul ye cannot reach, not if ye would.’ But they were greatly angered at the bold speech of the man, and they dislocated his hands and his feet with their dislocating engines, and wrenched his limbs out of their sockets, and unstrung them; and they twisted round his fingers, and his arms, and his legs, and his elbow-joints. And in no wise being able to strangle his spirit they stripped off his skin, taking the points of the fingers with it, and tore in Scythian fashion the scalp from his head, and straightway brought him to the wheel. And on this they twisted his spine till he saw his own flesh hanging in strips and great gouts of blood pouring down from his entrails. And at the point of death he said, ‘We, O most abominable tyrant, suffer thus for our upbringing and our virtue that are of Elohim (God); but thou for thy impiety and thy cruelty shalt endure torments without end.’* (4 Maccabees 10:1) The Messiah (Christ) tells his own that those who kill the body cannot kill the soul, and that the fear belongs to the One who holds both. The third of the seven brothers says the same thing to Antiochus on the wheel: apply every engine to this body, for my soul you cannot reach. He is of the seed of Yahudah (Judah), dying for keeping the Law of his fathers, and he tells the tyrant that his own cruelty will meet torments without end. The persecutor's power stops at the body, and the soul is held in another hand.$n$,$n$And when he had bravely met his glorious death, the third son was brought forward and was earnestly entreated by many to taste and so to save himself. But he answered in a loud voice, ‘Are ye ignorant that the same father begat me and my brothers that are dead, and the same mother gave us birth, and in the same doctrines was I brought up? I do not forswear the noble bond of brotherhood. Therefore if ye have any engine of torment, apply it to this body of mine; for my soul ye cannot reach, not if ye would.’ But they were greatly angered at the bold speech of the man, and they dislocated his hands and his feet with their dislocating engines, and wrenched his limbs out of their sockets, and unstrung them; and they twisted round his fingers, and his arms, and his legs, and his elbow-joints. And in no wise being able to strangle his spirit they stripped off his skin, taking the points of the fingers with it, and tore in Scythian fashion the scalp from his head, and straightway brought him to the wheel. And on this they twisted his spine till he saw his own flesh hanging in strips and great gouts of blood pouring down from his entrails. And at the point of death he said, ‘We, O most abominable tyrant, suffer thus for our upbringing and our virtue that are of Elohim (God); but thou for thy impiety and thy cruelty shalt endure torments without end.’$n$,true),
(4449,'T','4-maccabees',15,1,'4-maccabees',15,1,$n$*And fear not them which kill the body, but are not able to kill the soul: but rather fear him which is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.* (Matthew 10:28) *O Reason of the sons, lord over the passions! O religion, that wast dearer to the mother than her children! The mother, having two choices before her, religion and the present saving alive of her seven sons according to the tyrant's promise, loved rather religion, which saveth unto eternal life according to Elohim (God). O how may I express the passionate love of parents for children? We stamp a marvellous likeness of our soul and of our shape on the tender nature of the child, and most of all through the mother's sympathy with her children being deeper than the father's. For women are softer of soul than men, and the more children they bear the more do they abound in love for them.* (4 Maccabees 15:1) The Messiah (Christ) sets the order of fear plainly: do not fear those who can only reach the body, but the One who has power over soul and body. The mother of the seven brothers had that choice laid before her in the flesh, her sons' lives saved for the present on the tyrant's word, or religion which saves unto eternal life according to Elohim (God). She loved religion more, and she and her sons kept the Law of Yahuah (God) unto death. It is the same priority the Messiah (Christ) gives his own when he sends them out to be hated.$n$,$n$O Reason of the sons, lord over the passions! O religion, that wast dearer to the mother than her children! The mother, having two choices before her, religion and the present saving alive of her seven sons according to the tyrant's promise, loved rather religion, which saveth unto eternal life according to Elohim (God). O how may I express the passionate love of parents for children? We stamp a marvellous likeness of our soul and of our shape on the tender nature of the child, and most of all through the mother's sympathy with her children being deeper than the father's. For women are softer of soul than men, and the more children they bear the more do they abound in love for them.$n$,true),
(4450,'T','4-maccabees',17,13,'4-maccabees',17,12,$n$*And ye shall be hated of all men for my name’s sake: but he that endureth to the end shall be saved.* (Matthew 10:22) *For on that day virtue, proving them through endurance, set before them the prize of victory in incorruption in everlasting life.* (4 Maccabees 17:12) The Messiah (Christ) tells his own they will be hated of all men for his name, and that salvation belongs to the one who endures to the end. The book says of the seven brothers, Eleazar and their mother that on the day of trial virtue proved them through endurance and set before them the prize of victory in incorruption and everlasting life. Both put endurance first and the prize after it. These were the seed of Yahudah (Judah) holding to the Law unto death, and the life held out to them is life that does not decay, which the faithful asleep await with us at the resurrection.$n$,$n$For on that day virtue, proving them through endurance, set before them the prize of victory in incorruption in everlasting life.$n$,true),
(4451,'T','4-maccabees',17,17,'4-maccabees',17,17,$n$*And ye shall be hated of all men for my name’s sake: but he that endureth to the end shall be saved.* (Matthew 10:22) *The tyrant himself and his whole council admired their endurance, whereby they now do both stand beside the throne of Elohim (God) and live the blessed age.* (4 Maccabees 17:17) The Messiah (Christ) tells his own they will be hated of all men and that the one who endures to the end shall be saved. The book gives the seven brothers as the picture of that endurance: even the tyrant and his council admired it, and the book says it has brought them to stand beside the throne of Elohim (God) and live the blessed age. Endurance in the Law of their fathers did not end in the grave. The faithful who have fallen asleep wait with us for the first resurrection and are not made perfect without us, so what the verse holds out is the end promised to the one who endures.$n$,$n$The tyrant himself and his whole council admired their endurance, whereby they now do both stand beside the throne of Elohim (God) and live the blessed age.$n$,true),
(4452,'T','4-maccabees',10,4,'4-maccabees',10,4,$n$*For it is not ye that speak, but the Spirit of your Father which speaketh in you.* (Matthew 10:20) *But he said, ‘Even if thou dost remove my organ of speech, Elohim (God) is a hearer also of the speechless.* (4 Maccabees 10:4) The Messiah (Christ) tells his own not to take thought how or what they shall speak when they are brought before rulers, because the Spirit of their Father speaks in them. The fourth of the seven brothers faces the same hour when Antiochus orders his tongue cut out, and he answers that Elohim (God) is a hearer also of the speechless. What matters in the hour of trial does not depend on a prepared speech or even on the tongue, since the Father hears and is present with the one who stands for his Law.$n$,$n$But he said, ‘Even if thou dost remove my organ of speech, Elohim (God) is a hearer also of the speechless.$n$,true),
(4858,'T','4-maccabees',9,1,'4-maccabees',9,1,$n$*And he went a little further, and fell on his face, and prayed, saying, O my Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me: nevertheless not as I will, but as thou wilt.* (Matthew 26:39) *'Why dost thou delay, O tyrant? We are ready to die rather than transgress the commandments of our fathers.* (4 Maccabees 9:1) Both stand before a death they could avoid and choose the will of Elohim (God) over their own preservation. The seven brothers, the seed of Yahudah (Judah), tell the tyrant they are ready to die rather than transgress the commandments of their fathers, so they witness that Torah stands to the death. The Messiah (Christ) in the garden prays that the cup pass from him and then gives himself to the Father's will, nevertheless not as I will, but as thou wilt. The brothers' endurance foreshadows his laying down of his life, though the cup he takes is far greater than their pain, for he drinks it as the Pesach (Passover) lamb in obedience to the Father.$n$,$n$'Why dost thou delay, O tyrant? We are ready to die rather than transgress the commandments of our fathers.$n$,true),
(4859,'T','4-maccabees',17,13,'4-maccabees',17,15,$n$*He went away again the second time, and prayed, saying, O my Father, if this cup may not pass away from me, except I drink it, thy will be done.* (Matthew 26:42) *And righteousness won the victory, and gave the crown to her athletes.* (4 Maccabees 17:15) The Messiah (Christ) prays a second time that if the cup cannot pass he will drink it, and he ends with thy will be done, the Son's willing submission to the Father who sent him. The book says of the seven brothers that righteousness won the victory and gave the crown to her athletes, because they would not turn from the Law even to save their lives. Their crown is the foreshadow, and his drinking of the cup leads to the resurrection on the third day. Obedience to the Father's will through death is the road to the prize in both.$n$,$n$And righteousness won the victory, and gave the crown to her athletes.$n$,true),
(4860,'T','4-maccabees',17,17,'4-maccabees',17,19,$n$*Rise, let us be going: behold, he is at hand that doth betray me.* (Matthew 26:46) *And these men, therefore, having sanctified themselves for Elohim's (God’s) sake, not only have received this honour, but also the honour that through them the enemy had no more power over our people, and the tyrant suffered punishment, and our country was purified, they having as it were become a ransom for our nation’s sin; and through the blood of these righteous men and the propitiation of their death, the divine Providence delivered Yashar'el (Israel) that before was evil entreated.* (4 Maccabees 17:19) The Messiah (Christ) rises from his prayer and walks toward the one who betrays him, not away from him, because the hour of his laying down his life is at hand. The book, in its own voice, says of the seven brothers that they became as it were a ransom for the nation's sin, and that through their death the divine Providence delivered Yashar'el (Israel). That is the book's witness, and it foreshadows the Messiah's (Christ's) laying down of his life rather than replacing it, for the brothers show that Torah stands to the death. The cross is the door home to the commandments, and the Messiah (Christ) goes to it willingly as the Pesach (Passover) lamb.$n$,$n$And these men, therefore, having sanctified themselves for Elohim's (God’s) sake, not only have received this honour, but also the honour that through them the enemy had no more power over our people, and the tyrant suffered punishment, and our country was purified, they having as it were become a ransom for our nation’s sin; and through the blood of these righteous men and the propitiation of their death, the divine Providence delivered Yashar'el (Israel) that before was evil entreated.$n$,true),
(4873,'T','4-maccabees',15,1,'4-maccabees',15,1,$n$*He saved others; himself he cannot save. If he be the King of Yashar'el (Israel), let him now come down from the cross, and we will believe him.* (Matthew 27:42) *O Reason of the sons, lord over the passions! O religion, that wast dearer to the mother than her children! The mother, having two choices before her, religion and the present saving alive of her seven sons according to the tyrant's promise, loved rather religion, which saveth unto eternal life according to Elohim (God). O how may I express the passionate love of parents for children? We stamp a marvellous likeness of our soul and of our shape on the tender nature of the child, and most of all through the mother's sympathy with her children being deeper than the father's. For women are softer of soul than men, and the more children they bear the more do they abound in love for them.* (4 Maccabees 15:1) The mockers at the cross offer the choice of a present deliverance: come down and we will believe. It is the choice the mother of the seven brothers had before her, the saving alive of her sons for the present or religion which saves unto eternal life according to Elohim (God), and she loved religion more. The Messiah (Christ) could have saved himself and did not, for his life was not taken from him; he laid it down of himself. He chooses the Father's purpose over present safety, as she chose the Law of Yahuah (God) over the tyrant's promise.$n$,$n$O Reason of the sons, lord over the passions! O religion, that wast dearer to the mother than her children! The mother, having two choices before her, religion and the present saving alive of her seven sons according to the tyrant's promise, loved rather religion, which saveth unto eternal life according to Elohim (God). O how may I express the passionate love of parents for children? We stamp a marvellous likeness of our soul and of our shape on the tender nature of the child, and most of all through the mother's sympathy with her children being deeper than the father's. For women are softer of soul than men, and the more children they bear the more do they abound in love for them.$n$,true),
(4874,'T','4-maccabees',7,12,'4-maccabees',7,12,$n$*He trusted in Elohim (God); let him deliver him now, if he will have him: for he said, I am the Son of Elohim (God).* (Matthew 27:43) *But as many as with their whole heart make righteousness their first thought, these alone are able to master the weakness of the flesh, believing that unto Elohim (God) they die not, as our patriarchs, Abraham and Isaac and Jacob, died not, but that they live unto Elohim (God).* (4 Maccabees 7:12) The mockers say that since he trusted in Elohim (God), let him deliver him now. The book answers from the other side: those who make righteousness their first thought believe that they die not unto Elohim (God), as Abraham, Isaac and Jacob died and yet live unto him. Deliverance for the one who trusts is not measured by whether he comes down from the cross. The fathers sleep and await the first resurrection with us, and Yahusha's (Jesus's) deliverance came on the third day.$n$,$n$But as many as with their whole heart make righteousness their first thought, these alone are able to master the weakness of the flesh, believing that unto Elohim (God) they die not, as our patriarchs, Abraham and Isaac and Jacob, died not, but that they live unto Elohim (God).$n$,true),
(4875,'T','4-maccabees',17,13,'4-maccabees',17,12,$n$*Yahusha (Jesus), when he had cried again with a loud voice, yielded up the ghost.* (Matthew 27:50) *For on that day virtue, proving them through endurance, set before them the prize of victory in incorruption in everlasting life.* (4 Maccabees 17:12) Yahusha (Jesus) cries with a loud voice and yields up the ghost, laying down his life of his own will, as he had said no one takes it from him. The book says that on the day of trial virtue proved the seven brothers through endurance and set before them the prize of victory in incorruption in everlasting life. The brothers endured in the Law unto death, and their endurance foreshadows his. The prize set before him is the resurrection on the third day and the first resurrection at his coming.$n$,$n$For on that day virtue, proving them through endurance, set before them the prize of victory in incorruption in everlasting life.$n$,true),
(4876,'T','4-maccabees',17,17,'4-maccabees',17,17,$n$*Yahusha (Jesus), when he had cried again with a loud voice, yielded up the ghost.* (Matthew 27:50) *The tyrant himself and his whole council admired their endurance, whereby they now do both stand beside the throne of Elohim (God) and live the blessed age.* (4 Maccabees 17:17) *And these men, therefore, having sanctified themselves for Elohim's (God’s) sake, not only have received this honour, but also the honour that through them the enemy had no more power over our people, and the tyrant suffered punishment, and our country was purified, they having as it were become a ransom for our nation’s sin; and through the blood of these righteous men and the propitiation of their death, the divine Providence delivered Yashar'el (Israel) that before was evil entreated.* (4 Maccabees 17:19) Yahusha (Jesus) yields up the ghost, laying down his life of his own will. The book shows the seven brothers admired even by the tyrant and now standing beside the throne of Elohim (God), and in its own voice says their death was as it were a ransom for the nation's sin. That is the book's witness, and it foreshadows the Messiah's (Christ's) laying down of his life rather than standing in its place, for what the brothers prove is that Torah stands to the death. The cross is the door home to the commandments, and he gives his life once, as the Pesach (Passover) lamb.$n$,$n$The tyrant himself and his whole council admired their endurance, whereby they now do both stand beside the throne of Elohim (God) and live the blessed age.$n$,true),
(63397,'S','4-maccabees',2,5,'4-maccabees',2,5,$n$Deuteronomy 30:19 — *I call heaven and earth to record this day against you, that I have set before you life and death, blessing and cursing: therefore choose life, that both thou and thy seed may live.* The Law given "to the mind" so a man may reign over a temperate and just kingdom is the choice of life Moses sets before Israel.$n$,$n$For in the day when Elohim (God) created man, he implanted in him his passions and inclinations, and also, at the very same time, set the mind on a throne amidst the senses to be his sacred guide in all things; and to the mind he gave the Law, by the which if a man order himself, he shall reign over a kingdom that is temperate, and just, and virtuous, and brave.$n$,false),
(63407,'S','4-maccabees',2,2,'4-maccabees',2,2,$n$Leviticus 19:9 — *And when ye reap the harvest of your land, thou shalt not wholly reap the corners of thy field, neither shalt thou gather the gleanings of thy harvest.* To be one who "refrains from gleaning his stubbles" is to obey the law of the corners and the gleanings left for the poor.$n$,$n$How else can a man, naturally gormandizing and greedy and drunken, be taught to change his nature, if the Reason be not manifestly the master of the passions? Certainly, as soon as a man orders his life according to the Law, if he is miserly he acts contrary to his nature, and lends money to the needy without interest, and at the seventh-year periods cancels the debt. And if he is parsimonious, he is overruled by the Law through the action of Reason, and refrains from gleaning his stubbles or picking the last grapes from his vineyards.$n$,false),
(63464,'S','4-maccabees',6,6,'4-maccabees',6,6,$n$Hebrews 9:14 — *the blood of Messiah (Christ), who through the eternal Spirit offered himself without spot to Elohim (God), purge your conscience from dead works* — the blood that truly purifies, which Eleazar's *make my blood their purification* pointed toward.$n$,$n$So they, seeing him thus triumphant over the tortures and unmoved even by the pity of his executioners, dragged him to the fire. There they cast him on it, burning him with cruelly cunning devices, and they poured broth of evil odour into his nostrils. But when the fire already reached to his bones and he was about to give up the ghost, he lifted up his eyes to Elohim (God) and said, 'Thou, O Elohim (God), knowest that though I might save myself I am dying by fiery torments for thy Law. Be merciful unto thy people, and let our punishment be a satisfaction in their behalf. Make my blood their purification, and take my soul to ransom their souls.'$n$,false),
(63469,'S','4-maccabees',6,6,'4-maccabees',6,6,$n$Isaiah 53:10 — *when thou shalt make his soul an offering for sin, he shall see his seed, he shall prolong his days* — the soul given as an offering for sin, which the martyr's surrendered soul only pointed toward.$n$,$n$So they, seeing him thus triumphant over the tortures and unmoved even by the pity of his executioners, dragged him to the fire. There they cast him on it, burning him with cruelly cunning devices, and they poured broth of evil odour into his nostrils. But when the fire already reached to his bones and he was about to give up the ghost, he lifted up his eyes to Elohim (God) and said, 'Thou, O Elohim (God), knowest that though I might save myself I am dying by fiery torments for thy Law. Be merciful unto thy people, and let our punishment be a satisfaction in their behalf. Make my blood their purification, and take my soul to ransom their souls.'$n$,false),
(63470,'S','4-maccabees',6,6,'4-maccabees',6,6,$n$Isaiah 53:5 — *he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed* — the Suffering Servant whose pattern Eleazar's blood-plea dimly reflects, a pattern that waited for its fulfilment.$n$,$n$So they, seeing him thus triumphant over the tortures and unmoved even by the pity of his executioners, dragged him to the fire. There they cast him on it, burning him with cruelly cunning devices, and they poured broth of evil odour into his nostrils. But when the fire already reached to his bones and he was about to give up the ghost, he lifted up his eyes to Elohim (God) and said, 'Thou, O Elohim (God), knowest that though I might save myself I am dying by fiery torments for thy Law. Be merciful unto thy people, and let our punishment be a satisfaction in their behalf. Make my blood their purification, and take my soul to ransom their souls.'$n$,false),
(63517,'S','4-maccabees',9,13,'4-maccabees',9,13,$n$Matthew 10:18 — *And ye shall be brought before governors and kings for my sake, for a testimony against them and the Gentiles.* The youth confessing before the tyrant that he suffers for defending the Law of Elohim is the standing before kings as a testimony that the Messiah foretold.$n$,$n$'O thou most abominable tyrant, thou enemy of the justice of heaven and bloody-minded, thou dost torment me in this fashion not for manslaying nor for impiety but for defending the Law of Elohim (God).'$n$,false),
(63524,'S','4-maccabees',9,2,'4-maccabees',9,2,$n$Deuteronomy 30:16 — *I command thee this day to love Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God), to walk in his ways, and to keep his commandments and his statutes and his judgments, that thou mayest live and multiply.* Their resolve to walk in obedience to the Law and take Moses as counsellor is the walking in his ways that Moses commanded for life.$n$,$n$For we should be putting our ancestors also to shame, if we did not walk in obedience to the Law and take Moses as our counsellor.$n$,false),
(63552,'S','4-maccabees',13,12,'4-maccabees',13,12,$n$1 John 3:16 — *Hereby perceive we the love of Elohim (God), because he laid down his life for us: and we ought to lay down our lives for the brethren* — the laying down of life for one's brethren that the seven enacted to the death, brotherly love bred under the Law.$n$,$n$The feeling of brotherly love being thus naturally strong, the seven brethren had their mutual concord made yet stronger. For trained in the same Law, and disciplined in the same virtues, and brought up together in the upright life, they loved one another the more abundantly. Their common zeal for moral beauty and goodness heightened their mutual concord, for in conjunction with their piety it rendered their brotherly love more fervent.$n$,false),
(63607,'S','4-maccabees',17,15,'4-maccabees',17,15,$n$2 Timothy 4:8 — *Henceforth there is laid up for me a crown of righteousness, which Yahusha (Lord), the righteous judge, shall give me at that day: and not to me only, but unto all them also that love his appearing.* — the crown of righteousness given by the Judge, as righteousness *gave the crown to her athletes.*$n$,$n$And righteousness won the victory, and gave the crown to her athletes.$n$,false),
(63619,'S','4-maccabees',17,19,'4-maccabees',17,19,$n$Isaiah 53:5 — *But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed* — the Suffering Servant whose wounded blood truly delivers, which the martyrs' death only pointed toward.$n$,$n$And these men, therefore, having sanctified themselves for Elohim's (God’s) sake, not only have received this honour, but also the honour that through them the enemy had no more power over our people, and the tyrant suffered punishment, and our country was purified, they having as it were become a ransom for our nation’s sin; and through the blood of these righteous men and the propitiation of their death, the divine Providence delivered Yashar'el (Israel) that before was evil entreated.$n$,false),
(63622,'S','4-maccabees',17,19,'4-maccabees',17,19,$n$Isaiah 53:12 — *he hath poured out his soul unto death... and he bare the sin of many, and made intercession for the transgressors* — the One who pours out his soul and bears the nation's sin, the one whom the martyrs' blood foreshadows.$n$,$n$And these men, therefore, having sanctified themselves for Elohim's (God’s) sake, not only have received this honour, but also the honour that through them the enemy had no more power over our people, and the tyrant suffered punishment, and our country was purified, they having as it were become a ransom for our nation’s sin; and through the blood of these righteous men and the propitiation of their death, the divine Providence delivered Yashar'el (Israel) that before was evil entreated.$n$,false),
(63625,'S','4-maccabees',17,19,'4-maccabees',17,19,$n$Hebrews 9:14 — *the blood of Messiah, who through the eternal Spirit offered himself without spot to Elohim, purge your conscience from dead works* — the blood that truly cleanses the people, which the martyrs' blood only pointed toward.$n$,$n$And these men, therefore, having sanctified themselves for Elohim's (God’s) sake, not only have received this honour, but also the honour that through them the enemy had no more power over our people, and the tyrant suffered punishment, and our country was purified, they having as it were become a ransom for our nation’s sin; and through the blood of these righteous men and the propitiation of their death, the divine Providence delivered Yashar'el (Israel) that before was evil entreated.$n$,false),
(63655,'S','4-maccabees',18,20,'4-maccabees',18,20,$n$Daniel 12:2 — *and many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life* — the resurrection of the just, the *pure and immortal souls* the sons of Abraham received from Elohim.$n$,$n$But the sons of Abraham, with their victorious mother, are gathered together unto the place of their ancestors, having received pure and immortal souls from Elohim (God), to whom be glory for ever and ever. Amen.$n$,false),
(69292,'S','adam-eve',1,1,'adam-eve',1,1,$n$Genesis 4:2 — *And she again bare his brother Abel. And Abel was a keeper of sheep, but Cain was a tiller of the ground.* The two sons Eve bears in Adam and Eve 1:1, Cain and Abel, are the same pair Moses names in their birth order.$n$,$n$This is the story of Adam and Eve after they had gone out of Paradise. And Adam knew his wife Eve and went upwards to the sun-rising and abode there eighteen years and two months. And Eve conceived and bare two sons; Adiaphotos, who is called Cain and Amilabes who is called Abel.$n$,false),
(69295,'S','adam-eve',3,1,'adam-eve',3,1,$n$1 John 3:12 — *Not as Cain, who was of that wicked one, and slew his brother. And wherefore slew he him? Because his own works were evil, and his brother's righteous.* The hand of Cain in Adam and Eve 3:1 is the very fratricide John sets as the warning against hatred.$n$,$n$And they both went and found Abel murdered. I by the hand of Cain his brother. And Elohim (God) saith to Michael the archangel: 'Say to Adam: " Reveal not the secret that thou knowest to Cain thy son, for he is a son of wrath. But grieve not, for I will give thee another son in his stead; he shall show (to thee) all that thou shalt do. Do thou tell him nothing."' Thus spake the archangel to Adam. But he kept the word in his heart, and with him also Eve, though they grieved concerning Abel their son.$n$,false),
(69296,'S','adam-eve',3,1,'adam-eve',3,1,$n$Hebrews 11:4 — *By faith Abel offered unto Elohim (God) a more excellent sacrifice than Cain, by which he obtained witness that he was righteous, Elohim (God) testifying of his gifts: and by it he being dead yet speaketh.* The murdered Abel of Adam and Eve 3:1 is the righteous one whose blood still speaks.$n$,$n$And they both went and found Abel murdered. I by the hand of Cain his brother. And Elohim (God) saith to Michael the archangel: 'Say to Adam: " Reveal not the secret that thou knowest to Cain thy son, for he is a son of wrath. But grieve not, for I will give thee another son in his stead; he shall show (to thee) all that thou shalt do. Do thou tell him nothing."' Thus spake the archangel to Adam. But he kept the word in his heart, and with him also Eve, though they grieved concerning Abel their son.$n$,false),
(69298,'S','adam-eve',3,1,'adam-eve',3,1,$n$Genesis 4:8 — *And Cain talked with Abel his brother: and it came to pass, when they were in the field, that Cain rose up against Abel his brother, and slew him.* Abel found murdered by his brother's hand in Adam and Eve 3:1 is the slaying Moses records in the field.$n$,$n$And they both went and found Abel murdered. I by the hand of Cain his brother. And Elohim (God) saith to Michael the archangel: 'Say to Adam: " Reveal not the secret that thou knowest to Cain thy son, for he is a son of wrath. But grieve not, for I will give thee another son in his stead; he shall show (to thee) all that thou shalt do. Do thou tell him nothing."' Thus spake the archangel to Adam. But he kept the word in his heart, and with him also Eve, though they grieved concerning Abel their son.$n$,false),
(69301,'S','adam-eve',4,1,'adam-eve',4,1,$n$Genesis 5:3 — *And Adam lived an hundred and thirty years, and begat a son in his own likeness, after his image; and called his name Seth.* The Seth born to Adam in Adam and Eve 4:1 is the son begotten in Adam's own likeness who heads the book of the generations.$n$,$n$And after this, Adam knew Eve his wife, and she conceived and bare Seth. And Adam said to Eve: 'See! we have begotten a son in place of Abel, whom Cain slew, let us give glory and sacrifice to Elohim (God).'$n$,false),
(69302,'S','adam-eve',4,1,'adam-eve',4,1,$n$Genesis 4:25 — *And Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For Elohim (God), said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew.* Adam's word in Adam and Eve 4:1, a son in place of Abel whom Cain slew, is Moses' account verbatim of the appointed seed.$n$,$n$And after this, Adam knew Eve his wife, and she conceived and bare Seth. And Adam said to Eve: 'See! we have begotten a son in place of Abel, whom Cain slew, let us give glory and sacrifice to Elohim (God).'$n$,false),
(69304,'S','adam-eve',5,1,'adam-eve',5,1,$n$Genesis 5:5 — *And all the days that Adam lived were nine hundred and thirty years: and he died.* The nine hundred and thirty years and the sickness unto death in Adam and Eve 5:1 are the exact span Moses sets down for the first man.$n$,$n$And Adam begat thirty sons and thirty daughters and Adam lived nine hundred and thirty years; and he fell sick and cried with a loud voice and said, 'Let all my sons come to me that I may see them before I die.'$n$,false),
(69306,'S','adam-eve',7,1,'adam-eve',7,1,$n$Genesis 2:17 — *But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die.* The one tree forbidden and the death through it in Adam and Eve 7:1 are the single command and its sentence Moses set down.$n$,$n$And Adam said to him: 'When Elohim (God) made us, me and your mother, through whom also I die, He gave us power to eat of every tree which is in paradise, but, concerning that one only, He charged us not to eat of it, and through this one we are to die. And the hour drew nigh for the angels who were guarding your mother to go up and worship Yahuah (Lord), and I was far from her, and the enemy knew that she was alone and gave to her, and she ate of the tree of which she had been told not to eat.$n$,false),
(69308,'S','adam-eve',8,1,'adam-eve',8,1,$n$Psalms 139:12 — *Yea, the darkness hideth not from thee; but the night shineth as the day: the darkness and the light are both alike to thee.* The house cannot hide from its builder, says Adam and Eve 8:1; no darkness hides the creature from the Maker, says the psalm.$n$,$n$'And Elohim (God) was wroth with us, and Yahuah (Lord) came into paradise and called me in a terrible voice and said: "Adam, where art thou And why hidest thou from my face Shall the house be able to hide itself from its builder" And he saith to me: "Since thou hast abandoned my covenant, I have brought upon thy body seventy-two strokes; the trouble of the first stroke is a pain of the eyes, the second stroke an affection of the hearing, and likewise in turn all the strokes shall befall thee."'$n$,false),
(69310,'S','adam-eve',8,1,'adam-eve',8,1,$n$Genesis 3:9 — *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) called unto Adam, and said unto him, Where art thou?* The terrible call "Adam, where art thou" in Adam and Eve 8:1 is the very word Moses records first after the Fall.$n$,$n$'And Elohim (God) was wroth with us, and Yahuah (Lord) came into paradise and called me in a terrible voice and said: "Adam, where art thou And why hidest thou from my face Shall the house be able to hide itself from its builder" And he saith to me: "Since thou hast abandoned my covenant, I have brought upon thy body seventy-two strokes; the trouble of the first stroke is a pain of the eyes, the second stroke an affection of the hearing, and likewise in turn all the strokes shall befall thee."'$n$,false),
(69311,'S','adam-eve',8,1,'adam-eve',8,1,$n$Genesis 3:8 — *And they heard the voice of Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and Adam and his wife hid themselves from the presence of Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) amongst the trees of the garden.* Yahuah coming into paradise and the man hiding in Adam and Eve 8:1 is Moses' scene of the voice in the garden.$n$,$n$'And Elohim (God) was wroth with us, and Yahuah (Lord) came into paradise and called me in a terrible voice and said: "Adam, where art thou And why hidest thou from my face Shall the house be able to hide itself from its builder" And he saith to me: "Since thou hast abandoned my covenant, I have brought upon thy body seventy-two strokes; the trouble of the first stroke is a pain of the eyes, the second stroke an affection of the hearing, and likewise in turn all the strokes shall befall thee."'$n$,false),
(69314,'S','adam-eve',9,1,'adam-eve',9,1,$n$Genesis 3:17 — *And unto Adam he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life.* Adam's distress and toils in Adam and Eve 9:1 are the sorrow Moses laid on the man for hearkening to his wife.$n$,$n$As he said this to his sons, Adam groaned sore and said: 'What shall I do I am in great distress.' And Eve wept and said: 'My lord Adam, rise up and give me half of thy trouble and I will endure it; for it is on my account that this hath happened to thee, on my account thou art beset with toils and troubles.' But Adam said to Eve, 'Arise and go with my son Seth near to paradise, and put earth upon your heads and weep and pray Elohim (God) to have mercy upon me and send his angel to paradise, and give me of the tree out of which the oil floweth, and bring it me, and I shall anoint myself and shall have rest from my complaint.'$n$,false),
(69319,'S','adam-eve',12,1,'adam-eve',10,1,$n$Genesis 9:2 — *And the fear of you and the dread of you shall be upon every beast of the earth, and upon every fowl of the air... into your hand are they delivered.* The subjection of the beasts, fractured in Eden so that the beast now "fights with the image of Elohim," is the dominion renewed to Noah.$n$,$n$Then Seth and Eve went towards paradise, and Eve saw her son, and a wild beast assailing him, and Eve wept and said: 'Woe is me; if I come to the day of the Resurrection, all those who have sinned will curse me saying: Eve hath not kept the commandment of Elohim (God).' And she spake to the beast: 'Thou wicked beast, fearest thou not to fight with the image of Elohim (God) How was thy mouth opened How were thy teeth made strong How didst thou not call to mind thy subjection For long ago wast thou made subject to the image of Elohim (God).' Then the beast cried out and said:$n$,false),
(69320,'S','adam-eve',12,1,'adam-eve',10,1,$n$Genesis 1:28 — *And Elohim (God) blessed them... and have dominion over the fish of the sea, and over the fowl of the air, and over every living thing that moveth upon the earth.* The plea that the beast "was made subject to the image of Elohim" recalls the dominion granted at creation.$n$,$n$Then Seth and Eve went towards paradise, and Eve saw her son, and a wild beast assailing him, and Eve wept and said: 'Woe is me; if I come to the day of the Resurrection, all those who have sinned will curse me saying: Eve hath not kept the commandment of Elohim (God).' And she spake to the beast: 'Thou wicked beast, fearest thou not to fight with the image of Elohim (God) How was thy mouth opened How were thy teeth made strong How didst thou not call to mind thy subjection For long ago wast thou made subject to the image of Elohim (God).' Then the beast cried out and said:$n$,false),
(69336,'S','adam-eve',19,1,'adam-eve',19,1,$n$James 1:15 — *Then when lust hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin: and sin, when it is finished, bringeth forth death.* James spells out the chain Adam and Eve 19:1 names lust 'the root and beginning of every sin' that ends in death.$n$,$n$And I opened to him and he walked a little way, then turned and said to me: "I have changed my mind and I will not give thee to eat until thou swear to me to give also to thy husband." (And) I said. "What sort of oath shall I swear to thee Yet what I know, I say to thee: By the throne of the Master, and by the Cherubim and the Tree of Life! I will give also to my husband to eat." And when he had received the oath from me, he went and poured upon the fruit the poison of his wickedness, which is lust, the root and beginning of every sin, and he bent the branch on the earth and I took of the fruit and I ate.$n$,false),
(69339,'S','adam-eve',20,1,'adam-eve',20,1,$n$Genesis 3:7 — *And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; and they sewed fig leaves together, and made themselves aprons.* Eve's eyes opened and her flight to leaves (Adam and Eve 20:1,4-5, the fig tree alone) is this very verse told from within.$n$,$n$And in that very hour my eyes were opened, and forthwith I knew that I was bare of the righteousness with which I had been clothed (upon), and I wept and said to him: "Why hast thou done this to me in that thou hast deprived me of the glory with which I was clothed" But I wept also about the oath, which I had sworn. But he descended from the tree and vanished.$n$,false),
(69342,'S','adam-eve',22,1,'adam-eve',22,1,$n$1 Thessalonians 4:16 — *For Yahusha (Lord) himself shall descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with the trump of Elohim (God): and the dead in Messiah (Christ) shall rise first:* The archangel and trumpet heralding Yahuah's coming in Eden is the same figure Paul uses for the descent at the last day.$n$,$n$And in that same hour, we heard the archangel Michael blowing with his trumpet and calling to the angels and saying: "Thus saith Yahuah (Lord), Come with me to Paradise and hear the judgement with which I shall judge Adam."$n$,false),
(69345,'S','adam-eve',22,2,'adam-eve',22,2,$n$Genesis 3:8 — *And they heard the voice of Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and Adam and his wife hid themselves from the presence of Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) amongst the trees of the garden.* Elohim's appearing in paradise to judge Adam (Adam and Eve 22:1–2) is this coming of Yahuah into the garden, dramatised with trumpet and chariot.$n$,$n$And when Elohim (God) appeared in paradise, mounted on the chariot of his cherubim with the angels proceeding before him and singing hymns of praises, all the plants of paradise, both of your father's lot and mine, broke out into flowers. And the throne of Elohim (God) was fixed where the Tree of Life was.$n$,false),
(69352,'S','adam-eve',24,1,'adam-eve',24,1,$n$Genesis 3:17 — *And unto Adam he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life;* The 'hearkened to thy wife' and 'cursed is the earth' of Adam and Eve 24:1 is this verse.$n$,$n$Elohim (God) saith to Adam: 'Since thou hast disregarded my commandment and hast hearkened to thy wife, cursed is the earth in thy labours. Thou shalt work it and it shall not give its strength: thorns and thistles shall spring up for thee, and in the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat thy bread. [Thou shalt be in manifold toils; thou shalt be crushed by bitterness, but of sweetness shalt thou not taste.]$n$,false),
(69358,'S','adam-eve',26,2,'adam-eve',26,2,$n$Revelation 12:9 — *And the great dragon was cast out, that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world: he was cast out into the earth, and his angels were cast out with him.* 'That old serpent' is the same enemy of Adam and Eve 26:2, his head now bruised by being cast down.$n$,$n$Thou shalt be deprived of the victual of which thou didst eat and shalt feed on dust all the days of thy life: on thy breast and thy belly shalt thou walk and be robbed of hands and feet. There shall not be left thee ear nor wing, nor one limb of all that with which thou didst ensnare them in thy malice and causedst them to be cast out of paradise; and I will put enmity between thee and his seed: he shall bruise thy head and thou shalt bruise his heel until the day of Judgement."$n$,false),
(69383,'S','adam-eve',36,1,'adam-eve',36,1,$n$Revelation 6:12 — *And I beheld when he had opened the sixth seal, and, lo, there was a great earthquake; and the sun became black as sackcloth of hair, and the moon became as blood.* The hidden light of the luminaries is John's sun turned black as sackcloth and blood-red moon at the great judgment.$n$,$n$And Seth telleth his mother, that they are the sun and moon and themselves fall down and pray on behalf of my father Adam. Eve saith to him: 'And where is their light and why have they taken on such a black appearance' And Seth answereth her, 'The light hath not left them, but they cannot shine before the Light of the Universe, the Father of Light; and on this account their light hath been hidden from them.$n$,false),
(69405,'S','adam-eve',41,1,'adam-eve',41,1,$n$1 Corinthians 15:22 — *For as in Adam all die, even so in Messiah (Christ) shall all be made alive.* Raising Adam with every man of his seed is Paul's word that in Adam all die and in Messiah (Christ) all are made alive — the same root and the same harvest.$n$,$n$And Elohim (God) called and said, 'Adam, Adam.' And the body answered from the earth and said: 'Here am I, Yahuah (Lord).' And Elohim (God) saith to him: 'I told thee (that) earth thou art and to earth shalt thou return. Again I promise to thee the Resurrection; I will raise thee up in the Resurrection with every man who is of thy seed.'$n$,false),
(69411,'S','adam-eve',42,2,'adam-eve',42,2,$n$Ephesians 5:31 — *For this cause shall a man leave his father and mother, and shall be joined unto his wife, and they two shall be one flesh.* Eve's plea that they not be separated is Paul's one-flesh union carried from Genesis.$n$,$n$And Eve also, when the six days were fulfilled, fell asleep. But while she was living, she wept bitterly about Adam's falling on sleep, for she knew not where he was laid. For when Yahuah (Lord) came to paradise to bury Adam she was asleep, and her sons too, except Seth, till He bade Adam be prepared for burial; and no man knew on earth, except her son Seth. And Eve prayed (in the hour of her death) that she might be buried in the place where her husband Adam was. And after she had finished her prayer, she saith: 'Lord, Master, Elohim (God) of all rule, estrange not me thy handmaid from the body of Adam, for from his members didst thou make me. But deem me worthy, even me unworthy that I am and a sinner, to enter into his tabernacle, even as I was with him in paradise, both without separation from each other; just as in our transgression, we were (both) led astray and transgressed thy command, but were not separated. Even so, Yahuah (Lord), do not separate us now.' But after she had prayed, she gazed heavenwards and groaned aloud and smote her breast and said: 'Elohim (God) of All, receive my spirit,' and straightway she delivered up her spirit to Elohim (God).$n$,false),
(69433,'S','adam-eve',56,1,'adam-eve',56,1,$n$Hebrews 1:6 — *And again, when he bringeth in the firstbegotten into the world, he saith, And let all the angels of Elohim (God) worship him.* The command that Michael and the angels worship the image of Elohim in Adam and Eve 56:1 finds its true end in the firstbegotten, whom all the angels of Elohim are bidden to worship.$n$,$n$The devil replied, 'Adam, what dost thou tell me It is for thy sake that I have been hurled from that place. When thou wast formed. I was hurled out of the presence of Elohim (God) and banished from the company of the angels. When Elohim (God) blew into thee the breath of life and thy face and likeness was made in the image of Elohim (God), Michael also brought thee and made (us) worship thee in the sight of Elohim (God); and Elohim (God) Yahuah (Lord) spake: Here is Adam. I have made thee in our image and likeness.'$n$,false),
(69434,'S','adam-eve',56,1,'adam-eve',56,1,$n$Colossians 1:15 — *Who is the image of the invisible Elohim (God), the firstborn of every creature.* The image of Elohim borne by the formed man in Adam and Eve 56:1 has its perfect pattern in the Son, the true image of the unseen Elohim.$n$,$n$The devil replied, 'Adam, what dost thou tell me It is for thy sake that I have been hurled from that place. When thou wast formed. I was hurled out of the presence of Elohim (God) and banished from the company of the angels. When Elohim (God) blew into thee the breath of life and thy face and likeness was made in the image of Elohim (God), Michael also brought thee and made (us) worship thee in the sight of Elohim (God); and Elohim (God) Yahuah (Lord) spake: Here is Adam. I have made thee in our image and likeness.'$n$,false),
(69435,'S','adam-eve',56,1,'adam-eve',56,1,$n$Genesis 2:7 — *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life; and man became a living soul.* The breath of life the devil says was blown into Adam in Adam and Eve 56:1 is the breath Moses says Yahuah Elohim breathed into the man's nostrils.$n$,$n$The devil replied, 'Adam, what dost thou tell me It is for thy sake that I have been hurled from that place. When thou wast formed. I was hurled out of the presence of Elohim (God) and banished from the company of the angels. When Elohim (God) blew into thee the breath of life and thy face and likeness was made in the image of Elohim (God), Michael also brought thee and made (us) worship thee in the sight of Elohim (God); and Elohim (God) Yahuah (Lord) spake: Here is Adam. I have made thee in our image and likeness.'$n$,false),
(69436,'S','adam-eve',56,1,'adam-eve',56,1,$n$Genesis 1:27 — *So Elohim (God) created man in his own image, in the image of Elohim (God) created he him; male and female created he them.* The image of Elohim that the angels were bidden to honour in Adam and Eve 56:1 is the image Moses says was stamped upon the man at his creation.$n$,$n$The devil replied, 'Adam, what dost thou tell me It is for thy sake that I have been hurled from that place. When thou wast formed. I was hurled out of the presence of Elohim (God) and banished from the company of the angels. When Elohim (God) blew into thee the breath of life and thy face and likeness was made in the image of Elohim (God), Michael also brought thee and made (us) worship thee in the sight of Elohim (God); and Elohim (God) Yahuah (Lord) spake: Here is Adam. I have made thee in our image and likeness.'$n$,false),
(69438,'S','adam-eve',58,1,'adam-eve',58,1,$n$1 Enoch 6:2 — *And the angels, the children of the heaven, saw and lusted after them, and said to one another: 'Come, let us choose us wives from among the children of men and beget us children.'* The angels who rebelled with the adversary in Adam and Eve 58:1 are remembered in the book of the fathers as the Watchers, the children of heaven who forsook their estate.$n$,$n$When the angels, who were under me, heard this, they refused to worship him. And Michael saith, 'Worship the image of Elohim (God), but if thou wilt not worship him, Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) will be wrath with thee.' And I said, 'If He be wrath with me, I will set my seat above the stars of heaven and will be like the Highest.'$n$,false),
(69439,'S','adam-eve',58,1,'adam-eve',59,1,$n$Revelation 12:9 — *And the great dragon was cast out, that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world: he was cast out into the earth, and his angels were cast out with him.* The expulsion onto the earth that the devil mourns in Adam and Eve 59:1 is the casting-out of the dragon and his angels into the earth that John saw.$n$,$n$And Elohim (God) Yahuah (Lord) was wrath with me and banished me and my angels from our glory; and on thy account were we expelled from our abodes into this world and hurled on the earth. And straightway we were overcome with grief, since we had been spoiled of so great glory. And we were grieved when we saw thee in such joy and luxury. And with guile I cheated thy wife and caused thee to be expelled through her (doing) from thy joy and luxury, as I have been driven out of my glory.$n$,false),
(69440,'S','adam-eve',58,1,'adam-eve',59,1,$n$Revelation 12:7 — *And there was war in heaven: Michael and his angels fought against the dragon; and the dragon fought and his angels.* The banishing of the adversary and his angels from glory in Adam and Eve 59:1 is the war in heaven where Michael and his angels war against the dragon and his.$n$,$n$And Elohim (God) Yahuah (Lord) was wrath with me and banished me and my angels from our glory; and on thy account were we expelled from our abodes into this world and hurled on the earth. And straightway we were overcome with grief, since we had been spoiled of so great glory. And we were grieved when we saw thee in such joy and luxury. And with guile I cheated thy wife and caused thee to be expelled through her (doing) from thy joy and luxury, as I have been driven out of my glory.$n$,false),
(69441,'S','adam-eve',58,1,'adam-eve',59,1,$n$Luke 10:18 — *And he said unto them, I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven.* The casting-out the devil confesses in Adam and Eve 59:1, that followed his refusal in 57:3, is the lightning-fall the Messiah testifies He beheld.$n$,$n$And Elohim (God) Yahuah (Lord) was wrath with me and banished me and my angels from our glory; and on thy account were we expelled from our abodes into this world and hurled on the earth. And straightway we were overcome with grief, since we had been spoiled of so great glory. And we were grieved when we saw thee in such joy and luxury. And with guile I cheated thy wife and caused thee to be expelled through her (doing) from thy joy and luxury, as I have been driven out of my glory.$n$,false),
(69442,'S','adam-eve',58,1,'adam-eve',58,1,$n$Ezekiel 28:17 — *Thine heart was lifted up because of thy beauty, thou hast corrupted thy wisdom by reason of thy brightness: I will cast thee to the ground, I will lay thee before kings, that they may behold thee.* The lifted-up heart that would not bow in Adam and Eve 57:1 is the corruption Ezekiel names in the covering cherub cast to the ground.$n$,$n$When the angels, who were under me, heard this, they refused to worship him. And Michael saith, 'Worship the image of Elohim (God), but if thou wilt not worship him, Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) will be wrath with thee.' And I said, 'If He be wrath with me, I will set my seat above the stars of heaven and will be like the Highest.'$n$,false),
(69443,'S','adam-eve',58,1,'adam-eve',58,1,$n$Ezekiel 28:15 — *Thou wast perfect in thy ways from the day that thou wast created, till iniquity was found in thee.* The angels under the adversary who joined his refusal in Adam and Eve 58:1 followed one in whom, as Ezekiel says, iniquity was found after his perfect beginning.$n$,$n$When the angels, who were under me, heard this, they refused to worship him. And Michael saith, 'Worship the image of Elohim (God), but if thou wilt not worship him, Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) will be wrath with thee.' And I said, 'If He be wrath with me, I will set my seat above the stars of heaven and will be like the Highest.'$n$,false),
(69444,'S','adam-eve',58,1,'adam-eve',58,1,$n$Isaiah 14:14 — *I will ascend above the heights of the clouds; I will be like the El Elyon (most High).* The devil's last word in Adam and Eve 58:1 — 'I will be like the Highest' — is the very claim Isaiah records, to be like the El Elyon.$n$,$n$When the angels, who were under me, heard this, they refused to worship him. And Michael saith, 'Worship the image of Elohim (God), but if thou wilt not worship him, Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) will be wrath with thee.' And I said, 'If He be wrath with me, I will set my seat above the stars of heaven and will be like the Highest.'$n$,false),
(69445,'S','adam-eve',58,1,'adam-eve',58,1,$n$Isaiah 14:13 — *For thou hast said in thine heart, I will ascend into heaven, I will exalt my throne above the stars of Elohim (God): I will sit also upon the mount of the congregation, in the sides of the north.* The boast of Adam and Eve 58:1 — to set his seat above the stars of heaven — is almost word for word the throne Isaiah's fallen one would exalt above the stars of Elohim.$n$,$n$When the angels, who were under me, heard this, they refused to worship him. And Michael saith, 'Worship the image of Elohim (God), but if thou wilt not worship him, Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) will be wrath with thee.' And I said, 'If He be wrath with me, I will set my seat above the stars of heaven and will be like the Highest.'$n$,false),
(69446,'S','adam-eve',58,1,'adam-eve',58,1,$n$Isaiah 14:12 — *How art thou fallen from heaven, O Lucifer, son of the morning! how art thou cut down to the ground, which didst weaken the nations!* The adversary's refusal in Adam and Eve 57:1 to honour his junior is the pride Isaiah laments in the one fallen from heaven.$n$,$n$When the angels, who were under me, heard this, they refused to worship him. And Michael saith, 'Worship the image of Elohim (God), but if thou wilt not worship him, Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) will be wrath with thee.' And I said, 'If He be wrath with me, I will set my seat above the stars of heaven and will be like the Highest.'$n$,false),
(69447,'S','adam-eve',61,1,'adam-eve',61,1,$n$Romans 5:14 — *Nevertheless death reigned from Adam to Moses, even over them that had not sinned after the similitude of Adam's transgression, who is the figure of him that was to come.* Though Eve takes the blame in Adam and Eve 61:1, Paul fixes the reign of death upon Adam's transgression — and names him the figure of Him that was to come.$n$,$n$And Eve said to Adam: 'Live thou, my Lord, to thee life is granted, since thou hast committed neither the first nor the second error. But I have erred and been led astray for I have not kept the commandment of Elohim (God); and now banish me from the light of thy life and I will go to the sunsetting, and there will I be, until I die.' And she began to walk towards the western parts and to mourn and to weep bitterly and groan aloud. And she made there a booth, while she had in her womb offspring of three months old.$n$,false),
(69448,'S','adam-eve',61,1,'adam-eve',61,1,$n$1 Timothy 2:14 — *And Adam was not deceived, but the woman being deceived was in the transgression.* Eve's confession in Adam and Eve 61:1, 'I have erred and been led astray,' is exactly Paul's account that the woman, being deceived, was in the transgression.$n$,$n$And Eve said to Adam: 'Live thou, my Lord, to thee life is granted, since thou hast committed neither the first nor the second error. But I have erred and been led astray for I have not kept the commandment of Elohim (God); and now banish me from the light of thy life and I will go to the sunsetting, and there will I be, until I die.' And she began to walk towards the western parts and to mourn and to weep bitterly and groan aloud. And she made there a booth, while she had in her womb offspring of three months old.$n$,false),
(69449,'S','adam-eve',61,1,'adam-eve',61,1,$n$1 Timothy 2:13 — *For Adam was first formed, then Eve.* Eve's word in Adam and Eve 61:1, that life is granted to Adam who erred neither first nor second, rests on the order Paul names — Adam first formed, then Eve.$n$,$n$And Eve said to Adam: 'Live thou, my Lord, to thee life is granted, since thou hast committed neither the first nor the second error. But I have erred and been led astray for I have not kept the commandment of Elohim (God); and now banish me from the light of thy life and I will go to the sunsetting, and there will I be, until I die.' And she began to walk towards the western parts and to mourn and to weep bitterly and groan aloud. And she made there a booth, while she had in her womb offspring of three months old.$n$,false),
(69450,'S','adam-eve',61,1,'adam-eve',61,1,$n$Genesis 3:6 — *And when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat.* The error Eve confesses in Adam and Eve 61:1, that she did not keep the commandment, is the taking and eating Moses records of the woman.$n$,$n$And Eve said to Adam: 'Live thou, my Lord, to thee life is granted, since thou hast committed neither the first nor the second error. But I have erred and been led astray for I have not kept the commandment of Elohim (God); and now banish me from the light of thy life and I will go to the sunsetting, and there will I be, until I die.' And she began to walk towards the western parts and to mourn and to weep bitterly and groan aloud. And she made there a booth, while she had in her womb offspring of three months old.$n$,false),
(69451,'S','adam-eve',64,1,'adam-eve',64,1,$n$Genesis 4:1 — *And Adam knew Eve his wife; and she conceived, and bare Cain, and said, I have gotten a man from Yahuah (LORD).* The son Eve bears and names Cain in Adam and Eve 64:1 is the very firstborn Moses sets at the head of Adam's generations.$n$,$n$And behold, there came twelve angels and two 'virtues', standing on the right and on the left of Eve; and Michael was standing on the right; and he stroked her on the face as far as to the breast and said to Eve: 'Blessed art thou, Eve, for Adam's sake. Since his prayers and intercessions are great, I have been sent that thou mayst receive our help. Rise up now, and prepare thee to bear. And she bore a son and he was shining; and at once the babe rose up and ran and bore a blade of grass in his hands, and gave it to his mother, and his name was called Cain.$n$,false),
(69452,'S','adam-eve',64,1,'adam-eve',62,1,$n$Genesis 3:16 — *Unto the woman he said, I will greatly multiply thy sorrow and thy conception; in sorrow thou shalt bring forth children; and thy desire shall be to thy husband, and he shall rule over thee.* The distress and pains that overtake Eve at her bearing in Adam and Eve 62:1 are the sorrow in childbearing Moses pronounced upon the woman.$n$,$n$And when the time of her bearing approached, she began to be distressed with pains, and she cried aloud to Yahuah (Lord) and said: 'Pity me, O Yahuah (Lord), assist me.' And she was not heard and the mercy of Elohim (God) did not encircle her. And she said to herself: 'Who shall tell my lord Adam I implore you, ye luminaries of heaven, what time ye return to the east, bear a message to my lord Adam.'$n$,false),
(69453,'S','adam-eve',66,2,'adam-eve',67,2,$n$Genesis 5:4 — *And the days of Adam after he had begotten Seth were eight hundred years: and he begat sons and daughters.* The eight hundred years and the sons and daughters Adam begets after Seth in Adam and Eve 67:2 are the very span and increase Moses records in the generations of Adam.$n$,$n$And after Adam had begotten Seth, he lived eight hundred years and begat thirty sons and thirty daughters; in all sixty-three children. And they were increased over the face of the earth in their nations.$n$,false),
(69454,'S','adam-eve',66,1,'adam-eve',67,1,$n$Genesis 4:25 — *And Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For Elohim (God), said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew.* Adam's word in Adam and Eve 67:1, that Seth is begotten in place of Abel whom Cain slew, is the very reckoning Moses sets at Seth's naming.$n$,$n$And Adam said to Eve, 'Behold, I have begotten a son, in place of Abel, whom Cain slew.'$n$,false),
(69455,'S','adam-eve',66,2,'adam-eve',66,2,$n$1 John 3:12 — *Not as Cain, who was of that wicked one, and slew his brother. And wherefore slew he him? Because his own works were evil, and his brother's righteous.* The murder told in Adam and Eve 66:2 is the deed John holds up as the pattern of hatred — Cain, of the wicked one, who slew his righteous brother.$n$,$n$And thereafter, Cain slew Abel, but Adam was then one hundred and thirty years old, but Abel was slain when he was one hundred and twenty-two years. And thereafter Adam knew his wife and he begat a son and called his name Seth.$n$,false),
(69456,'S','adam-eve',66,2,'adam-eve',66,2,$n$Genesis 4:8 — *And Cain talked with Abel his brother: and it came to pass, when they were in the field, that Cain rose up against Abel his brother, and slew him.* The slaying of Abel by Cain in Adam and Eve 66:2 is the murder in the field Moses records.$n$,$n$And thereafter, Cain slew Abel, but Adam was then one hundred and thirty years old, but Abel was slain when he was one hundred and twenty-two years. And thereafter Adam knew his wife and he begat a son and called his name Seth.$n$,false),
(69457,'S','adam-eve',66,1,'adam-eve',66,1,$n$Genesis 4:2 — *And she again bare his brother Abel. And Abel was a keeper of sheep, but Cain was a tiller of the ground.* The two callings made for the brothers in Adam and Eve 66:1, Cain the husbandman and Abel the shepherd, are the very trades Moses assigns them.$n$,$n$And they made Cain an husbandman, (but) Abel they made a shepherd; in order that in this wise they might be mutually separated.$n$,false),
(69526,'S','adam-eve',94,1,'adam-eve',94,1,$n$Revelation 14:13 — *And I heard a voice from heaven saying unto me, Write, Blessed are the dead which die in Yahusha (Lord) from henceforth: Yea, saith the Spirit, that they may rest from their labours; and their works do follow them.* The seventh day as the sign of the resurrection is the blessed rest of the dead in Yahuah — labour ended, the age to come begun.$n$,$n$When they had been mourning four days, (then) Michael the archangel appeared and said to Seth: 'Man of Elohim (God), mourn not for thy dead more than six days, for on the seventh day is the sign of the resurrection and the rest of the age to come; on the seventh day Yahuah (Lord) rested from all His works.'$n$,false);
INSERT INTO _s478m VALUES
(111,4448,$n$4 Maccabees 10:1 — *apply it to this body of mine; for my soul ye cannot reach, not if ye would.* The third brother tells Antiochus what Yahusha (Jesus) later tells his own, that the soul is beyond the persecutor's reach.$n$),
(111,4449,$n$4 Maccabees 15:1 — *loved rather religion, which saveth unto eternal life according to Elohim (God).* The mother chooses eternal life over the saving alive of her sons, the same order of fear Matthew 10:28 gives.$n$),
(112,4450,$n$4 Maccabees 17:12 — *virtue, proving them through endurance, set before them the prize of victory in incorruption in everlasting life.* Endurance is proved first and the prize of incorruption follows, as Matthew 10:22 says of the one who endures to the end.$n$),
(112,4451,$n$4 Maccabees 17:17 — *they now do both stand beside the throne of Elohim (God) and live the blessed age.* The brothers' endurance is shown as ending beside the throne of Elohim (God), the end Matthew 10:22 promises.$n$),
(113,4452,$n$4 Maccabees 10:4 — *Even if thou dost remove my organ of speech, Elohim (God) is a hearer also of the speechless.* The brother whose tongue is to be cut out trusts that Elohim (God) hears without words, as Matthew 10:20 promises the Father's Spirit speaks in his own.$n$),
(413,4858,$n$4 Maccabees 9:1 — *We are ready to die rather than transgress the commandments of our fathers.* The brothers' readiness to die rather than break the Law foreshadows the Messiah's (Christ's) submission to the Father's will in the garden.$n$),
(413,4859,$n$4 Maccabees 17:15 — *righteousness won the victory, and gave the crown to her athletes.* The brothers' crown for faithfulness unto death foreshadows the Messiah's (Christ's) obedience, thy will be done, that leads to the third day.$n$),
(413,4860,$n$4 Maccabees 17:19 — *they having as it were become a ransom for our nation’s sin.* The book's own words about a ransom foreshadow the Messiah's (Christ's) laying down of his life as he walks to the arrest.$n$),
(417,4873,$n$4 Maccabees 15:1 — *loved rather religion, which saveth unto eternal life according to Elohim (God).* The mother chose eternal life over saving her sons alive, the choice the mockers cannot see the Messiah (Christ) making.$n$),
(417,4874,$n$4 Maccabees 7:12 — *believing that unto Elohim (God) they die not, as our patriarchs, Abraham and Isaac and Jacob, died not.* Those who trust Elohim (God) live unto him even through death, which answers the mockers' demand for deliverance now.$n$),
(417,4875,$n$4 Maccabees 17:12 — *virtue, proving them through endurance, set before them the prize of victory in incorruption in everlasting life.* The brothers' endurance unto death with the prize of incorruption before them foreshadows the Messiah (Christ) yielding up his life.$n$),
(417,4876,$n$4 Maccabees 17:17 — *they now do both stand beside the throne of Elohim (God) and live the blessed age.* The brothers' endurance, which the book calls as it were a ransom in 17:19, foreshadows the Messiah's (Christ's) laying down of his life.$n$),
(15736,63517,$n$Matthew 10:18 — *And ye shall be brought before governors and kings for my sake, for a testimony against them and the Gentiles.* The youth confessing before the tyrant that he suffers for defending the Law of Elohim is the standing before kings as a testimony that the Messiah foretold.$n$),
(15735,63524,$n$Deuteronomy 30:16 — *I command thee this day to love Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God), to walk in his ways, and to keep his commandments and his statutes and his judgments, that thou mayest live and multiply.* Their resolve to walk in obedience to the Law and take Moses as counsellor is the walking in his ways that Moses commanded for life.$n$),
(15745,63552,$n$1 John 3:16 — *Hereby perceive we the love of Elohim (God), because he laid down his life for us: and we ought to lay down our lives for the brethren* — the laying down of life for one's brethren that the seven enacted to the death, brotherly love bred under the Law.$n$),
(15759,63655,$n$Daniel 12:2 — *and many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life* — the resurrection of the just, the *pure and immortal souls* the sons of Abraham received from Elohim.$n$),
(17357,69383,$n$Revelation 6:12 — *And I beheld when he had opened the sixth seal, and, lo, there was a great earthquake; and the sun became black as sackcloth of hair, and the moon became as blood.* The hidden light of the luminaries is John's sun turned black as sackcloth and blood-red moon at the great judgment.$n$),
(17362,69405,$n$1 Corinthians 15:22 — *For as in Adam all die, even so in Messiah (Christ) shall all be made alive.* Raising Adam with every man of his seed is Paul's word that in Adam all die and in Messiah (Christ) all are made alive — the same root and the same harvest.$n$),
(17363,69411,$n$Ephesians 5:31 — *For this cause shall a man leave his father and mother, and shall be joined unto his wife, and they two shall be one flesh.* Eve's plea that they not be separated is Paul's one-flesh union carried from Genesis.$n$),
(15727,63464,$n$Hebrews 9:14 — *the blood of Messiah (Christ), who through the eternal Spirit offered himself without spot to Elohim (God), purge your conscience from dead works* — the blood that truly purifies, which Eleazar's *make my blood their purification* pointed toward.$n$),
(15727,63469,$n$Isaiah 53:10 — *when thou shalt make his soul an offering for sin, he shall see his seed, he shall prolong his days* — the soul given as an offering for sin, which the martyr's surrendered soul only pointed toward.$n$),
(15727,63470,$n$Isaiah 53:5 — *he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed* — the Suffering Servant whose pattern Eleazar's blood-plea dimly reflects, a pattern that waited for its fulfilment.$n$),
(15755,63619,$n$Isaiah 53:5 — *But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed* — the Suffering Servant whose wounded blood truly delivers, which the martyrs' death only pointed toward.$n$),
(15755,63622,$n$Isaiah 53:12 — *he hath poured out his soul unto death... and he bare the sin of many, and made intercession for the transgressors* — the One who pours out his soul and bears the nation's sin, the one whom the martyrs' blood foreshadows.$n$),
(15755,63625,$n$Hebrews 9:14 — *the blood of Messiah, who through the eternal Spirit offered himself without spot to Elohim, purge your conscience from dead works* — the blood that truly cleanses the people, which the martyrs' blood only pointed toward.$n$),
(15713,63407,$n$Leviticus 19:9 — *And when ye reap the harvest of your land, thou shalt not wholly reap the corners of thy field, neither shalt thou gather the gleanings of thy harvest.* To be one who "refrains from gleaning his stubbles" is to obey the law of the corners and the gleanings left for the poor.$n$),
(15715,63397,$n$Deuteronomy 30:19 — *I call heaven and earth to record this day against you, that I have set before you life and death, blessing and cursing: therefore choose life, that both thou and thy seed may live.* The Law given "to the mind" so a man may reign over a temperate and just kingdom is the choice of life Moses sets before Israel.$n$),
(17371,69439,$n$Revelation 12:9 — *And the great dragon was cast out, that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world: he was cast out into the earth, and his angels were cast out with him.* The expulsion onto the earth that the devil mourns in Adam and Eve 59:1 is the casting-out of the dragon and his angels into the earth that John saw.$n$),
(17371,69440,$n$Revelation 12:7 — *And there was war in heaven: Michael and his angels fought against the dragon; and the dragon fought and his angels.* The banishing of the adversary and his angels from glory in Adam and Eve 59:1 is the war in heaven where Michael and his angels war against the dragon and his.$n$),
(17371,69441,$n$Luke 10:18 — *And he said unto them, I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven.* The casting-out the devil confesses in Adam and Eve 59:1, that followed his refusal in 57:3, is the lightning-fall the Messiah testifies He beheld.$n$),
(17373,69452,$n$Genesis 3:16 — *Unto the woman he said, I will greatly multiply thy sorrow and thy conception; in sorrow thou shalt bring forth children; and thy desire shall be to thy husband, and he shall rule over thee.* The distress and pains that overtake Eve at her bearing in Adam and Eve 62:1 are the sorrow in childbearing Moses pronounced upon the woman.$n$),
(17374,69453,$n$Genesis 5:4 — *And the days of Adam after he had begotten Seth were eight hundred years: and he begat sons and daughters.* The eight hundred years and the sons and daughters Adam begets after Seth in Adam and Eve 67:2 are the very span and increase Moses records in the generations of Adam.$n$),
(17374,69454,$n$Genesis 4:25 — *And Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For Elohim (God), said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew.* Adam's word in Adam and Eve 67:1, that Seth is begotten in place of Abel whom Cain slew, is the very reckoning Moses sets at Seth's naming.$n$),
(17331,69292,$n$Genesis 4:2 — *And she again bare his brother Abel. And Abel was a keeper of sheep, but Cain was a tiller of the ground.* The two sons Eve bears in Adam and Eve 1:1, Cain and Abel, are the same pair Moses names in their birth order.$n$),
(17332,69295,$n$1 John 3:12 — *Not as Cain, who was of that wicked one, and slew his brother. And wherefore slew he him? Because his own works were evil, and his brother's righteous.* The hand of Cain in Adam and Eve 3:1 is the very fratricide John sets as the warning against hatred.$n$),
(17332,69296,$n$Hebrews 11:4 — *By faith Abel offered unto Elohim (God) a more excellent sacrifice than Cain, by which he obtained witness that he was righteous, Elohim (God) testifying of his gifts: and by it he being dead yet speaketh.* The murdered Abel of Adam and Eve 3:1 is the righteous one whose blood still speaks.$n$),
(17332,69298,$n$Genesis 4:8 — *And Cain talked with Abel his brother: and it came to pass, when they were in the field, that Cain rose up against Abel his brother, and slew him.* Abel found murdered by his brother's hand in Adam and Eve 3:1 is the slaying Moses records in the field.$n$),
(17333,69301,$n$Genesis 5:3 — *And Adam lived an hundred and thirty years, and begat a son in his own likeness, after his image; and called his name Seth.* The Seth born to Adam in Adam and Eve 4:1 is the son begotten in Adam's own likeness who heads the book of the generations.$n$),
(17333,69302,$n$Genesis 4:25 — *And Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For Elohim (God), said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew.* Adam's word in Adam and Eve 4:1, a son in place of Abel whom Cain slew, is Moses' account verbatim of the appointed seed.$n$),
(17334,69304,$n$Genesis 5:5 — *And all the days that Adam lived were nine hundred and thirty years: and he died.* The nine hundred and thirty years and the sickness unto death in Adam and Eve 5:1 are the exact span Moses sets down for the first man.$n$),
(17335,69306,$n$Genesis 2:17 — *But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die.* The one tree forbidden and the death through it in Adam and Eve 7:1 are the single command and its sentence Moses set down.$n$),
(17336,69308,$n$Psalms 139:12 — *Yea, the darkness hideth not from thee; but the night shineth as the day: the darkness and the light are both alike to thee.* The house cannot hide from its builder, says Adam and Eve 8:1; no darkness hides the creature from the Maker, says the psalm.$n$),
(17336,69310,$n$Genesis 3:9 — *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) called unto Adam, and said unto him, Where art thou?* The terrible call "Adam, where art thou" in Adam and Eve 8:1 is the very word Moses records first after the Fall.$n$),
(17336,69311,$n$Genesis 3:8 — *And they heard the voice of Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and Adam and his wife hid themselves from the presence of Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) amongst the trees of the garden.* Yahuah coming into paradise and the man hiding in Adam and Eve 8:1 is Moses' scene of the voice in the garden.$n$),
(17337,69314,$n$Genesis 3:17 — *And unto Adam he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life.* Adam's distress and toils in Adam and Eve 9:1 are the sorrow Moses laid on the man for hearkening to his wife.$n$),
(17343,69336,$n$James 1:15 — *Then when lust hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin: and sin, when it is finished, bringeth forth death.* James spells out the chain Adam and Eve 19:1 names lust 'the root and beginning of every sin' that ends in death.$n$),
(17344,69339,$n$Genesis 3:7 — *And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; and they sewed fig leaves together, and made themselves aprons.* Eve's eyes opened and her flight to leaves (Adam and Eve 20:1,4-5, the fig tree alone) is this very verse told from within.$n$),
(17346,69345,$n$Genesis 3:8 — *And they heard the voice of Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and Adam and his wife hid themselves from the presence of Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) amongst the trees of the garden.* Elohim's appearing in paradise to judge Adam (Adam and Eve 22:1–2) is this coming of Yahuah into the garden, dramatised with trumpet and chariot.$n$),
(17348,69352,$n$Genesis 3:17 — *And unto Adam he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life;* The 'hearkened to thy wife' and 'cursed is the earth' of Adam and Eve 24:1 is this verse.$n$),
(17351,69358,$n$Revelation 12:9 — *And the great dragon was cast out, that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world: he was cast out into the earth, and his angels were cast out with him.* 'That old serpent' is the same enemy of Adam and Eve 26:2, his head now bruised by being cast down.$n$),
(17370,69433,$n$Hebrews 1:6 — *And again, when he bringeth in the firstbegotten into the world, he saith, And let all the angels of Elohim (God) worship him.* The command that Michael and the angels worship the image of Elohim in Adam and Eve 56:1 finds its true end in the firstbegotten, whom all the angels of Elohim are bidden to worship.$n$),
(17370,69434,$n$Colossians 1:15 — *Who is the image of the invisible Elohim (God), the firstborn of every creature.* The image of Elohim borne by the formed man in Adam and Eve 56:1 has its perfect pattern in the Son, the true image of the unseen Elohim.$n$),
(17370,69435,$n$Genesis 2:7 — *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life; and man became a living soul.* The breath of life the devil says was blown into Adam in Adam and Eve 56:1 is the breath Moses says Yahuah Elohim breathed into the man's nostrils.$n$),
(17370,69436,$n$Genesis 1:27 — *So Elohim (God) created man in his own image, in the image of Elohim (God) created he him; male and female created he them.* The image of Elohim that the angels were bidden to honour in Adam and Eve 56:1 is the image Moses says was stamped upon the man at his creation.$n$),
(17371,69438,$n$1 Enoch 6:2 — *And the angels, the children of the heaven, saw and lusted after them, and said to one another: 'Come, let us choose us wives from among the children of men and beget us children.'* The angels who rebelled with the adversary in Adam and Eve 58:1 are remembered in the book of the fathers as the Watchers, the children of heaven who forsook their estate.$n$),
(17371,69442,$n$Ezekiel 28:17 — *Thine heart was lifted up because of thy beauty, thou hast corrupted thy wisdom by reason of thy brightness: I will cast thee to the ground, I will lay thee before kings, that they may behold thee.* The lifted-up heart that would not bow in Adam and Eve 57:1 is the corruption Ezekiel names in the covering cherub cast to the ground.$n$),
(17371,69443,$n$Ezekiel 28:15 — *Thou wast perfect in thy ways from the day that thou wast created, till iniquity was found in thee.* The angels under the adversary who joined his refusal in Adam and Eve 58:1 followed one in whom, as Ezekiel says, iniquity was found after his perfect beginning.$n$),
(17371,69444,$n$Isaiah 14:14 — *I will ascend above the heights of the clouds; I will be like the El Elyon (most High).* The devil's last word in Adam and Eve 58:1 — 'I will be like the Highest' — is the very claim Isaiah records, to be like the El Elyon.$n$),
(17371,69445,$n$Isaiah 14:13 — *For thou hast said in thine heart, I will ascend into heaven, I will exalt my throne above the stars of Elohim (God): I will sit also upon the mount of the congregation, in the sides of the north.* The boast of Adam and Eve 58:1 — to set his seat above the stars of heaven — is almost word for word the throne Isaiah's fallen one would exalt above the stars of Elohim.$n$),
(17371,69446,$n$Isaiah 14:12 — *How art thou fallen from heaven, O Lucifer, son of the morning! how art thou cut down to the ground, which didst weaken the nations!* The adversary's refusal in Adam and Eve 57:1 to honour his junior is the pride Isaiah laments in the one fallen from heaven.$n$),
(17372,69447,$n$Romans 5:14 — *Nevertheless death reigned from Adam to Moses, even over them that had not sinned after the similitude of Adam's transgression, who is the figure of him that was to come.* Though Eve takes the blame in Adam and Eve 61:1, Paul fixes the reign of death upon Adam's transgression — and names him the figure of Him that was to come.$n$),
(17372,69448,$n$1 Timothy 2:14 — *And Adam was not deceived, but the woman being deceived was in the transgression.* Eve's confession in Adam and Eve 61:1, 'I have erred and been led astray,' is exactly Paul's account that the woman, being deceived, was in the transgression.$n$),
(17372,69449,$n$1 Timothy 2:13 — *For Adam was first formed, then Eve.* Eve's word in Adam and Eve 61:1, that life is granted to Adam who erred neither first nor second, rests on the order Paul names — Adam first formed, then Eve.$n$),
(17372,69450,$n$Genesis 3:6 — *And when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat.* The error Eve confesses in Adam and Eve 61:1, that she did not keep the commandment, is the taking and eating Moses records of the woman.$n$),
(17373,69451,$n$Genesis 4:1 — *And Adam knew Eve his wife; and she conceived, and bare Cain, and said, I have gotten a man from Yahuah (LORD).* The son Eve bears and names Cain in Adam and Eve 64:1 is the very firstborn Moses sets at the head of Adam's generations.$n$),
(17374,69455,$n$1 John 3:12 — *Not as Cain, who was of that wicked one, and slew his brother. And wherefore slew he him? Because his own works were evil, and his brother's righteous.* The murder told in Adam and Eve 66:2 is the deed John holds up as the pattern of hatred — Cain, of the wicked one, who slew his righteous brother.$n$),
(17374,69456,$n$Genesis 4:8 — *And Cain talked with Abel his brother: and it came to pass, when they were in the field, that Cain rose up against Abel his brother, and slew him.* The slaying of Abel by Cain in Adam and Eve 66:2 is the murder in the field Moses records.$n$),
(17374,69457,$n$Genesis 4:2 — *And she again bare his brother Abel. And Abel was a keeper of sheep, but Cain was a tiller of the ground.* The two callings made for the brothers in Adam and Eve 66:1, Cain the husbandman and Abel the shepherd, are the very trades Moses assigns them.$n$),
(17346,69342,$n$1 Thessalonians 4:16 — *For Yahusha (Lord) himself shall descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with the trump of Elohim (God): and the dead in Messiah (Christ) shall rise first:* The archangel and trumpet heralding Yahuah's coming in Eden is the same figure Paul uses for the descent at the last day.$n$),
(15753,63607,$n$2 Timothy 4:8 — *Henceforth there is laid up for me a crown of righteousness, which Yahusha (Lord), the righteous judge, shall give me at that day: and not to me only, but unto all them also that love his appearing.* — the crown of righteousness given by the Judge, as righteousness *gave the crown to her athletes.*$n$),
(17391,69526,$n$Revelation 14:13 — *And I heard a voice from heaven saying unto me, Write, Blessed are the dead which die in Yahusha (Lord) from henceforth: Yea, saith the Spirit, that they may rest from their labours; and their works do follow them.* The seventh day as the sign of the resurrection is the blessed rest of the dead in Yahuah — labour ended, the age to come begun.$n$);
